      "title": "iterable() incorrectly identifies objects with __getitem__ but broken __iter__ as iterable",
      "body": "The `iterable()` function uses `iter(obj)` which succeeds for objects implementing `__getitem__` (sequence protocol fallback). However, `astropy.wcs.WCS` implements `__getitem__` for slicing but its `__iter__` raises ValueError. This causes downstream code like `fixitems()` in figure.py to crash when calling `tuple(v)`. Fix `iterable()` to verify iteration actually works by attempting `next(iter(obj))` and catching exceptions."
    },
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "category": "bug",
      "path": "lib/matplotlib/figure.py",
      "line": 0,
      "title": "fixitems() crashes on objects that are iterable() but not tuple()-able",
      "body": "The `fixitems()` function assumes `iterable(v)` implies `tuple(v)` will succeed. This is false for objects like `astropy.wcs.WCS` where `iter()` succeeds (via `__getitem__` fallback) but iteration fails. Add a try/except around `tuple(v)` to gracefully handle non-tuple-able iterables, or use a more robust conversion that preserves the original object when tuple conversion fails."
    }
  ]
}

